f the heart to pump effectively, which can lead to fluid overload and hyponatremia\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n3522,PACIENTE,HIPONATREMIA,\"The patient is experiencing hyponatremia, which is the condition being treated\",612\r\n3072,PACIENTE,VALORACIÓN CLÍNICA,Clinical assessment is performed on the patient to evaluate their nutritional status,548\r\n4821,NUTRICIÓN PARENTERAL,HIPONATREMIA,\"NUTRICIÓN PARENTERAL refers to a method of providing nutrition to patients intravenously, bypassing the usual process of eating and digestion. Patients receiving parenteral nutrition are at a higher risk of developing HIPONATREMIA, a condition characterized by low sodium levels in the blood. This increased risk is primarily due to fluid overload and imbalances in sodium and other electrolytes that can occur during parenteral nutrition. Therefore, careful monitoring of fluid and electrolyte levels is essential for patients undergoing this type of nutritional support to prevent the onset of hiponatremia.\",504\r\n644,PACIENTES,HIPONATREMIA,\"Hyponatremia is a condition commonly observed in hospitalized patients, especially among those receiving artificial nutrition. These patients are diagnosed with hyponatremia, which indicates that they have low sodium levels in their blood. As a result, they require treatment to manage this condition effectively.\",293\r\n823,NUTRICIÓN,HIPONATREMIA,Nutrition management is crucial in treating hyponatremia,275\r\n1072,HOSPITAL,HIPONATREMIA,\"Hiponatremia has a high prevalence in hospital settings, affecting a significant percentage of hospitalized patients.\",162\r\n6816,NUTRICIÓN ARTIFICIAL,HIPONATREMIA,\"NUTRICIÓN ARTIFICIAL and HIPONATREMIA are closely related in the context of patient care, particularly for those receiving artificial nutrition. Hyponatremia, a condition characterized by low sodium levels in the blood, is frequently observed in patients undergoing artificial nutrition. This condition can arise due to fluid overload and impaired sodium regulation, as well as imbalances in electrolytes that may occur during the administration of artificial nutrition. \n\nIn some cases, hyponatremia may necessitate the use of artificial nutrition for management, especially in patients who are unable to maintain adequate sodium levels through a regular diet. Effective management of hyponatremia in these patients often requires careful adjustments to their artificial nutrition regimen to prevent complications. The diagnosis and treatment of hyponatremia in patients receiving artificial nutrition are critical areas of focus, highlighting the importance of monitoring and managing electrolyte intake to ensure patient safety and health.\",159\r\n7841,HIPONATREMIA,SIADH,\"Hyponatremia is a condition characterized by low sodium levels in the blood, and it is often a result of the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH). SIADH is a condition that leads to increased levels of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), which causes the body to retain excess water. This inappropriate secretion of ADH is a frequent cause of hyponatremia, as it results in water retention that dilutes sodium levels in the bloodstream. Thus, SIADH plays a significant role in the development of hyponatremia by promoting water retention and disrupting the balance of electrolytes.\",128\r\n7879,HIPONATREMIA,CIRROSIS,Cirrhosis can cause hyponatremia due to fluid retention and altered sodium balance,120\r\n3972,ESPANA,HIPONATREMIA,\"In Spain, the indirect method for measuring sodium levels is commonly used in patients with hyponatremia\",108\r\n7860,HIPONATREMIA,SODIO,\"Hyponatremia is characterized by low levels of sodium (sodio) in the body. Sodium levels are crucial for both the diagnosis and treatment of hyponatremia, highlighting the importance of monitoring and managing sodium levels in individuals affected by this condition.\",103\r\n7861,HIPONATREMIA,POTASIO